#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63426ad7-4275-5ac3-854d-e91ec00dcbe3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Trendline"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e5e5d8a-dcf6-5b1b-8fff-885e89e28ab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{2}x + 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148975} {:answer/id #uuid "b1aa10b9-88fe-5b75-9415-5f85307dfa2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3}{5}x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148976} {:answer/id #uuid "cc10671a-3fa6-52a1-a2e7-fc9e91c6da77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148977} {:answer/id #uuid "5ebd2902-1928-5e14-873f-1deefd3ebc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{3}{5}x + 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148978} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6a8cad-d9fe-5087-b9cf-34ce3f9d7d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{2}x + 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d6a8cad-d9fe-5087-b9cf-34ce3f9d7d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{2}x + 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148979} :answer-field/id #uuid "e31519f7-6422-5678-9d56-3fb5b548ac21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3646d88-0039-5610-b9ca-0659ca28504e" :question/math-academy-id "q-133764" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/f6cbd594db6ab2454844d744ce1b776c3f561c4a6295abb9222ad2416c9f9292.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,8)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(8,5).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-8}{8-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+b \\\\ 8 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(2)+b \\\\ 8 & =-1+b \\\\ 9 & =b \\\\ b & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "327d9a77-b2dd-57b1-b959-570684714bf9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Trendline of a Given Scatter Plot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3199f78e-71f5-5d52-a8bb-857194812e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290286} {:answer/id #uuid "e352ea8e-110d-5208-b2f4-db65aee1f2a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no linear trend in the data" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290287} {:answer/id #uuid "8664c7c9-bd00-50c7-a032-33818b2fff58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290288} {:answer/id #uuid "f5357d5d-f59c-5155-b9af-c04e17999370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290289} {:answer/id #uuid "08d27e2b-694e-5013-addc-4090aecfe1b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8664c7c9-bd00-50c7-a032-33818b2fff58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290288} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c86b25-8508-5b27-ae3c-bf0b15f25ef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d495c92-f887-5668-a001-304b0b353bdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-134002" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/80cb125155a1a55404f2257196560a4e563e75c12d4c87c24b0464e509d7a0fa.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" :question/worked-solution "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a straight line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/18cba39701d1ec8ead1281a68fad5dab2b1f584aa396eddcfa306c4961c6397e.png)\n\nWe see that the line $s$ best fits the given data set."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63426ad7-4275-5ac3-854d-e91ec00dcbe3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Trendline"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0c246b1-c992-5b8d-9100-2e2445bb3873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290293} {:answer/id #uuid "db36ddb0-75ca-5efa-aaaa-4708bf13a43e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290294} {:answer/id #uuid "8da67108-2ac1-5f4e-a49c-1a86dd6a2379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290295} {:answer/id #uuid "a979bc2b-baa7-51f5-a3d6-a1ab523153bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290296} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbca36b-7ac1-5de4-a1b1-69dea8659a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8da67108-2ac1-5f4e-a49c-1a86dd6a2379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290295} :answer-field/id #uuid "3582ab5a-e997-56cf-93d5-82b7fd486779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1a6666b-a452-503d-ac89-478427988482" :question/math-academy-id "q-134018" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/da0836fa401981d7a0a254a136036a0ead7e3be8eefff5ef3fdfb31070759f0a.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,8).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-3}{5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=\\frac{5}{4}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{4}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{4}x+b \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{5}{4}(1)+b \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{5}{4}+b \\\\ 12 & =5+4b \\\\ 7 & =4b \\\\ b & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "890526df-fd1a-5340-8732-efc0c1a1b0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Equation of a Trendline"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64fb8705-4a31-5fa1-9083-23f2629bc24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290300} {:answer/id #uuid "37fc585b-c185-5685-ac0b-075023d83c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290301} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e7cd9-7737-5468-8024-b70d5a27683f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290302} {:answer/id #uuid "42714853-3f4f-5e3d-bbee-15d83af70cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290303} {:answer/id #uuid "11017683-60b7-5bf6-a679-a92f5b4af3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e7cd9-7737-5468-8024-b70d5a27683f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290302} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad4749a-747a-5689-8d95-58b7083cfd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce4a4fff-994e-59d0-86ef-d63b0f28fbed" :question/math-academy-id "q-134025" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/c8f6d0161777412704a9a80c0336af04a6968454d421913055024b6d0cf11c71.png)\n\nEstimate the slope of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round your answer to the nearest $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/b50fd1636cc2e1bb9d150eaba9cc25a3ccedff77e607bf22853004332a7c4ddc.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(3,6)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(5,3).$\n\nTherefore, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{2} \\\\  & =-1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "890526df-fd1a-5340-8732-efc0c1a1b0ae" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Equation of a Trendline"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "883eddbf-117e-5ffd-9cbe-efe7a5a1ea7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290307} {:answer/id #uuid "c258ab7b-bce5-5168-a472-b548cc7fa410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290308} {:answer/id #uuid "995a2444-73cb-559a-ac3b-eabe38374d65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290309} {:answer/id #uuid "cd007cd3-aa6c-50df-b2d6-54602fc7eb8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290310} {:answer/id #uuid "3b04a345-9518-56aa-a698-40984322a053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd007cd3-aa6c-50df-b2d6-54602fc7eb8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290310} :answer-field/id #uuid "98c02a1b-6e00-58f4-a3b9-b78546a59445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abbe9ce3-7f4a-5a47-a8ba-73e6933dd3f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-134026" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/75f8a5bd247b1f792fd60640fbbd2f864359abe48110d59f1fbe0da00eae4ed5.png)\n\nEstimate the slope of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round your answer to the nearest $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010570/assets/384610cdc149e8b4eaa6df1d3eea1715dda88543c57679553875d226814f6cf7.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(-1,4)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-4}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
